<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenes
<br class="ltx_break">by Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data </h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Björn Möller
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zhengyang Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Malte Stelzer
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Thomas Graave
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Fabian Bettels
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Muaaz Ataya
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tim Fingscheidt
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Technische Universität Braunschweig,
Institute for Communications Technology
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">{bjoern.moeller, zhengyang.li, malte.stelzer, thomas.graave,</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">f.bettels, m.ataya, t.fingscheidt}@tu-bs.de</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent successful video generation systems that predict and create realistic automotive driving scenes from short video inputs assign tokenization, future state prediction (world model), and video decoding to dedicated models.
These approaches often utilize large models that require significant training resources, offer limited insight into design choices, and lack publicly available code and datasets.
In this work, we address these deficiencies and present OpenViGA, an open video generation system for automotive driving scenes.
Our contributions are:
Unlike several earlier works for video generation, such as GAIA-1, we provide a deep analysis of the three components of our system by separate quantitative and qualitative evaluation: Image tokenizer, world model, video decoder.
Second, we purely build upon powerful pre-trained open source models from various domains, which we fine-tune by publicly available automotive data (BDD100K) on GPU hardware at academic scale.
Third, we build a coherent video generation system by streamlining interfaces of our components.
Fourth, due to public availability of the underlying models and data, we allow full reproducibility.
Finally, we also publish our code and models on Github<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        https://github.com/ifnspaml/OpenViGA</span></span></span>.
For an image size of 256x256 at 4 fps we are able to predict realistic driving scene videos frame-by-frame with only one frame of algorithmic latency.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/files/system_overview.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:336/101;" width="336" height="101" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Proposed video generation system<span id="S1.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: It consists of an image tokenizer, encoding <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> input frames <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> into a latent representation of discrete tokens <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, a world model then predicting <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> subsequent latent image tokens <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a video decoder, generating <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> output frames <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Models are pre-trained, open-source, and then fine-tuned on public automotive data.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video generation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> have improved significantly due to recent advances in large language models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> and generative AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, enabling the creation of realistic visual content from limited input data.
There is a growing focus on world model-based systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, which learn structured representations of dynamic environments and enable the generation of long, temporally consistent video sequences through autoregressive next-token prediction.
Such systems typically operate in three stages: A tokenizer (TOK) encodes the input into discrete representations, a world model (WM) predicts a future token sequence, and finally a video decoder (VDEC) reconstructs the predicted token into video frames <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In autonomous driving, video generation offers a scalable approach to improve safety <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> by simulating diverse driving scenarios, including rare and harmful events such as evasive maneuvers, addressing the scarcity of critical training data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>.
Despite the demonstrated potential of video generation models such as GAIA-1 and <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">GAIA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, the lack of open-source implementations or training code, sparse description of architectures, and non-public datasets limit reproducibility.
In contrast, <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">we propose <span id="S1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">OpenViGA</span>, an open video generation system for automotive driving scenes, see Fig.<a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, based purely on open-source models and public data, allowing for full reproducibility.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Open-source foundation LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, having learned temporal prediction from vast corpora of text data, provide a powerful basis for WMs in video generation.
Extending them to multi-modal token prediction using image, video, and text data, enables them to model spatio-temporal structures.
However, training such a WM is demanding with respect to training time, dataset size, and computational resources.
Therefore, we build upon the pre-trained 7B parameter <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> model and employ low-rank adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> to efficiently fine-tune it.
For our image tokenizer and decoder, we also leverage a pre-trained <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> model.
However, both these open-source models were originally trained on general-domain data causing deficiencies for automotive video generation.
Accordingly, we transfer them to the automotive domain by fine-tuning on public automotive data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize the large public driving dataset BDD100K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> to fine-tune our TOK on its images, and our VDEC and WM on its videos.
We also evaluate on the official image data splits.
To process the dataset’s video content with limited GPU memory, we have to streamline all components towards a coherent system in terms of predicted token sequence length (WM), frame rate (WM), token per image (TOK), and input frame resolution (all).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we first build a video generation system for automotive driving scenes (OpenViGA) and provide insights into its components by qualitative and quantitative evaluation.
We investigate loss architectures for image tokenizer and image decoder fine-tuning, evaluate the entire system, and investigate the world model’s top-<math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> selection hyperparameter, controlling its creativity.
Second, we build upon general-purpose open-source models and fine-tune them on public automotive driving data, using limited GPU resources.
Third, we describe streamlining of individual open-source system components.
For full reproducibility, we publish our training and inference code.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image Autoencoder Models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autoencoder (AE) models learn an efficient representation of data by encoding the input into a lower-dimensional representation and decoding the original data as accurately as possible. AEs are applied in areas such as image compression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, anomaly detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> and denoising <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
Incorporating convolutional layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, regularization techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, or probabilistic inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, image AEs are widely used to compress images into a compact latent representation for efficient processing in subsequent models.
Introducing quantization into the latent space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> results in discrete representations that enhance image generation and interpretability.
Following leading video generation approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, we fine-tune a <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> to encode patches of input frames into discrete tokens, which are then processed by our world model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">World Models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">World models (WMs) estimate missing information and predict future states of dynamic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
By learning latent representations of the environment, WMs can facilitate reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, long-sequence predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and controllable video generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.
In autonomous driving, WMs enhance navigation and enable environment interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DriveGAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> introduces an LSTM-based prediction model.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DriverDreamer</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Drive-WM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GAIA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> deploy diffusion models as WMs to predict multi-view videos, conditioned on frames, actions and additional inputs such as bounding boxes or maps.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DriveDreamer-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> extends on this with an LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> for user-friendly trajectory planing.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ADriver-I</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> also leverages an LLM to predict action priors for a video diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> model.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GAIA-1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> frames future prediction as next-token prediction over image patch tokens using an autoregressive transformer as WM, with a diffusion model for decoding, showing strong qualitative results.
However, the authors use a proprietary dataset and do not publish code or model weights, nor quantitative results beyond loss curves.
<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We employ a pre-trained, open-source autoregressive transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> as our WM, adapt it to the automotive domain using public data, and, in contrast to prior works, publish our inference and training code as well as model weights.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Video Generation Models</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video generation models synthesize temporally consistent video sequences from input prompts.
While GAN-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> and autoregressive models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> show good results, recent video diffusion models (VDMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> further enhance temporal consistency and quality in generated videos.
However, training such VDMs requires extensive hardware resources. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accordingly, we decide for a lower complexity attractive frame-by-frame approach by extending our fine-tuned <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> image decoder with temporal input context and fine-tune it on video data.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/files/system_inference.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/160;" width="685" height="160" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Inference<span id="S2.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> of the proposed </span>video generation system<span id="S2.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: A sequence of <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> future frames <math id="S2.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}</annotation></semantics></math> is predicted based on <math id="S2.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> initial input images <math id="S2.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and a fixed text prompt <math id="S2.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. The tokenizer encodes the prompted image sequence <math id="S2.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> into a discrete token sequence <math id="S2.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The world model predicts future tokens <math id="S2.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}</annotation></semantics></math> for the video decoder to generate the corresponding image sequence <math id="S2.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="1.110em">ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}</annotation></semantics></math>. Details of the image tokenizer (TOK, i.e., ENC and VQ) are shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VQGAN ‣ 3.2 Open Source Models ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. World model (WM) details are shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>OpenViGA</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, our proposed video generation system predicts <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> future frames <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\check{x}}_{t}\in\mathbb{I}^{H\times W\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\check{x}}_{t}\in\mathbb{I}^{H\times W\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math>, of automotive driving scenes based on an input sequence <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}\!=\!(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{1}^{T}\!=\!(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> initial images, where <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\in\!\mathbb{I}^{H\!\times\!W\!\times\!3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕀</mi><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.102em" rspace="0.102em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.102em" rspace="0.102em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}\!\in\!\mathbb{I}^{H\!\times\!W\!\times\!3}</annotation></semantics></math>, with height <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, width <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, 3 color channels, and gray values normalized to the range <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}\!=\![-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}\!=\![-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, a fixed text prompt is utilized, see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Video Generation Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In the following, we describe the system components, the open-source models used, and our approach of streamlining and fine-tuning those models to automotive data.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>System Components</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image Tokenizer</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The image tokenizer (TOK) in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Video Generation Models ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> converts a preprocessed image <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to a latent representation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu})\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n\!=\!16\times 16\!=\!256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>16</mn><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mpadded><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!16\times 16\!=\!256</annotation></semantics></math> discrete tokens <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>. It consists of an encoder (ENC), which encodes an image into <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> latent space representations of an image patch, and a vector quantizer (VQ), which outputs token vectors <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> from a learnable codebook <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{CB}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐂𝐁</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{CB}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> being the codebook size and codevector dimension <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.
We deploy an ENC and VQ from a <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> as image tokenizer (TOK).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">World Model</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on prompted image tokens <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{1}^{T}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> initial frames and a text prompt <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> text tokens, the world model (WM) predicts the discrete tokens of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> future frames, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the prompted image tokens <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}\!=\!(\mathbf{z}_{t,\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> are discrete, a vector-index mapping converts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a codebook index sequence <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>, with indices <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\nu}\in\mathcal{K}\!=\!\{0,...,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\nu}\in\mathcal{K}\!=\!\{0,...,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and patch position <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu\in\{1,...,T\cdot n^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu\in\{1,...,T\cdot n^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> of these codebook vectors.
Also, end-of-image tokens (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\tau\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!0,\quad\tau\in\{1,2,...,T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\tau\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!0,\quad\tau\in\{1,2,...,T\}</annotation></semantics></math>), with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}\!=\!n\!+\!1\!=\!257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}\!=\!n\!+\!1\!=\!257</annotation></semantics></math>, are inserted as structural information after each block of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> codebook indices that represent a prompted image.
Focusing on video continuation, we fix the text prompt to <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">"You are a helpful assistant. USER: Generate a video of driving vehicles. ASSISTANT: <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>VISION<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span>”, which is byte-pair encoded to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> text tokens <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VISION<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span> marks the beginning of the codebook index sequence <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> tokenized initial ground-truth frames.
From its concatenated inputs (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{T\cdot n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>), the WM predicts <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> future frames in an autoregressive loop, selecting the next image token in each of the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\cdot n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\cdot n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> total iterations.
The next image token is selected by first predicting a probability distribution and then sampling from the top-<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> most probable entries.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Video Decoder</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The video decoder (VDEC) generates <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> frames <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{x}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from the world model’s temporal predictions <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>ˇ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\check{\mathbf{z}}_{T+1}^{T+N}\!=\!(\check{\mathbf{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> one frame at a time, using a bidirectional input context window over three frames.
We deploy a 3D CNN as VDEC, derived from our fine-tuned 2D <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> image decoder (DEC) by 3D central inflation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Open Source Models</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">VQGAN</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The vector-quantized generative adversarial network (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> integrates vector quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to generate high-quality images with compact latent representations.
Our approach builds on an open-source general-purpose <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> originally trained for 2.5M steps on diverse real-world images at 256×256 resolution.
The TOK module (ENC + VQ) has 59.2M parameters, about 20% of GAIA-1’s tokenizer, and the DEC block contains 87M.
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">However, deploying it for front-facing camera videos of automotive driving scenes, the model exhibits limited reconstruction quality.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/files/vqgan_training.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:679/199;" width="679" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning<span id="S3.F3.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> of the <span id="S3.F3.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> </span>image tokenizer<span id="S3.F3.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>image decoder<span id="S3.F3.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> for a single training image <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (selectors in positions <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math>, as drawn). The image tokenizer consists of an encoder (ENC) and a vector quantizer (VQ) and is jointly trained with the image decoder (DEC), as part of a <span id="S3.F3.10.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> model. ENC and DEC are optimized by a combination of reconstruction loss <math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, GAN loss (generator component <math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and self-supervised learning loss <math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The VQ’s codebook <math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{CB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐂𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{CB}</annotation></semantics></math> and ENC are additionally optimized with a codebook loss <math id="S3.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{CB}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>CB</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{CB}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The </span>discriminator<span id="S3.F3.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> is alternatingly optimized on the same batch (selector positions <math id="S3.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast\ast</annotation></semantics></math>), using discriminator loss <math id="S3.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LWM</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The large world model (<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> is a 7B-parameter multimodal autoregressive decoder-only transformer based on <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> capable of processing sequences up to 1M tokens of both video and text modalities with 32 decoder blocks.
As our WM, we adopt the open-source LWM-Chat-1M model, originally trained on text and four vision-language tasks with VQGAN-encoded inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and 495B tokens from diverse web datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. For details, see Supplement A.
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">However, no dedicated driving video dataset containing recordings from forward-facing cameras was utilized.
Also, the task of predicting future latent frames based on initial latent frames was not addressed in </span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> training.
Moreover, </span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> was trained with 256 tokens per image defining the image tokenizer output size and thus limits the video frame resolution, which we have to streamline to fit to the BDD100K automotive dataset.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Streamlining</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before separately fine-tuning the pre-trained models, we streamline the components within the system for processing automotive data with limited hardware resources.
While the <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span> is capable of processing long token sequences yet with highly parallelized hardware, in practice, sequence length is limited by available GPU memory.
The open-source <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM-Chat-1M</span> was originally trained on video frames quantized to 256 tokens each and a frame rate of 4 fps, both of which we retain for our fine-tuning.
On a single <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Nvidia A100</span> GPU with 80GB of VRAM, we were able to predict image tokens 4 seconds into the future during fine-tuning, yielding a total predicted sequence length of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\cdot n^{\prime}\!=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\cdot n^{\prime}\!=</annotation></semantics></math> 4,112 tokens, which is the product of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=4</annotation></semantics></math> fps<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math> 4 <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s\!=\!16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s\!=\!16</annotation></semantics></math> frames, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}\!=\!n+1\!=257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo>+</mo></mpadded></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}\!=\!n+1\!=257</annotation></semantics></math> image tokens including a manually inserted end-of-image token.
Given that automotive datasets typically provide frames at a resolution of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.280\times 720" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.280</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>720</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.280\times 720</annotation></semantics></math> pixels, the 256-token per-frame constraint imposed by the world model presents a substantial challenge for tokenizing spatial features across the entire scene.
To address this, we first select an image tokenizer (as part of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span>) that allows for a relatively high input image size of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H\times W\times 3\!=\!256\times 256\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mpadded><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\times W\times 3\!=\!256\times 256\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> producing <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{H\!=\!256}{16}\times\frac{W\!=\!256}{16}\!=\!256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.158em">=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.158em">=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{H\!=\!256}{16}\times\frac{W\!=\!256}{16}\!=\!256</annotation></semantics></math> image patch tokens.
To grasp a meaningful section of the automotive scene, we downscale the original BDD100K image <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by a factor of 0.5 in height and width using bilinear interpolation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
This consequently increases the density of spatial detail, which adds difficulty in learning precise reconstructions during <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> fine-tuning.
The streamlined system takes a normalized image crop <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H\times W\times 3\!=\!256\times 256\times 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi><mpadded style="width:0.608em;" width="0.608em"><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mpadded><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\times W\times 3\!=\!256\times 256\times 3</annotation></semantics></math> from the image center as input, modeling the relevant portion of the driving scene.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Also, for later <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> fine-tuning (shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VQGAN ‣ 3.2 Open Source Models ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), a teacher network is used to distill relevant information into the discrete tokens <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by self-supervised learning (SSL).
To match the dimensions for the pre-trained <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span>’s quantized representations <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the teacher’s encoded latent representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a fully connected adapter layer <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FC(384)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>FC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>384</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FC(384)}</annotation></semantics></math> is inserted.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune the image tokenizer (TOK) jointly with the image decoder (DEC) as parts of a <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> model utilizing a combination of various loss functions, see Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VQGAN ‣ 3.2 Open Source Models ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As the TOK compresses each image independently, we fine-tune on an <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image</span> training dataset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>BDDimg</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and present the process for a single raw image <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is first downscaled, cropped, and normalized, yielding our ground-truth training image <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The TOK and DEC optimization can be expressed as total loss with hyperparameters <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}\!=\!J^{\mathrm{rec}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{CB}}J^{\mathrm{CB}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{SSL}}J^{\mathrm{SSL}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}J^{\mathrm{G}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>CB</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>CB</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}\!=\!J^{\mathrm{rec}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{CB}}J^{\mathrm{CB}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{SSL}}J^{\mathrm{SSL}}+\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}J^{\mathrm{G}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our reconstruction loss combines the pixel-wise <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the perceptual loss <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\prime}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\prime}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> in a weighted sum</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{rec}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\lambda_{1}||\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}||_{2}^{2}+\lambda^{\prime}J^{\prime}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{rec}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\lambda_{1}||\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}-\mathbf{x}_{t}||_{2}^{2}+\lambda^{\prime}J^{\prime}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The perceptual loss encourages reconstructions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that are perceptually similar to the reference image <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by similar feature activation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\mathbf{x}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\mathbf{x}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> for an ImageNet-pre-trained loss network <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> with</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\prime}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\sum_{\ell}\|\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})-\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></munder><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\prime}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=\sum_{\ell}\|\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})-\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\ell}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> refers to specific model layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The codebook <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CB</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CB}</annotation></semantics></math> is optimized using an embedding loss that utilizes the L<sub id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_sub">2</sub> error to align the codebook vectors and the encoder output <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while the commitment loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> forces the encoder to stick to a particular embedding, jointly forming the codebook loss</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{CB}}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t})=\underbrace{\|\mathrm{sg}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t})-\mathbf{z}_{t}\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{embedding loss}}+\beta\cdot\underbrace{\|\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}-\mathrm{sg}(\mathbf{z}_{t})\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{commitment loss}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>CB</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>embedding loss</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>commitment loss</mtext></munder></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{CB}}(\mathbf{z}_{t},\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t})=\underbrace{\|\mathrm{sg}(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t})-\mathbf{z}_{t}\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{embedding loss}}+\beta\cdot\underbrace{\|\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}-\mathrm{sg}(\mathbf{z}_{t})\|_{2}^{2}}_{\text{commitment loss}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sg}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>sg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sg}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stop-gradient operator and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\!=\!0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\!=\!0.25</annotation></semantics></math> is a commitment weight.
As the quantization function (VQ) is non-differentiable, gradients of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> are backwards copied from <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, influencing the codebook only indirectly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Also, a teacher model is leveraged to distill semantic meaning into the image patch tokens <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by enforcing cosine similarity between a teacher’s encoding <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the quantized and transformed representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, serving as a self-supervised learning loss</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}(\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}})=1-\frac{(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{\mathrm{SSL}}_{t})^{\mathrm{T}}\cdot\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}}{\mathrm{max}(\|\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}\|_{2}\cdot\|\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}\|_{2},\epsilon)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}(\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t},\mathbf{z}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}})=1-\frac{(\tilde{\mathbf{z}}^{\mathrm{SSL}}_{t})^{\mathrm{T}}\cdot\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}}{\mathrm{max}(\|\tilde{\mathbf{z}}_{t}^{\mathrm{SSL}}\|_{2}\cdot\|\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}\|_{2},\epsilon)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\!=\!10^{-8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\!=\!10^{-8}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{384}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>384</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime\prime}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{384}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of a learnable fully connected layer with input <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{64}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>64</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}^{\prime}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{64}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We adopt <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ViT-s DINOv2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> as the teacher, extending GAIA-1’s use of <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINOv1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, with experimental validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To generate realistic-looking images, a GAN loss approach is adopted, consisting of two loss components with complementary objectives for discriminator <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D(\&gt;)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D(\&gt;)}</annotation></semantics></math> and generator (TOK+DEC).
Included in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is the generator loss</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})=-\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})=-\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">depicted with selector positions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast</annotation></semantics></math> in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VQGAN ‣ 3.2 Open Source Models ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which optimizes the generator (TOK+DEC) to produce fake images, misleading the discriminator <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D(\&gt;)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D(\&gt;)}</annotation></semantics></math> into classifying them as real.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Complementing the total loss <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), a patch-based discriminator <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> is trained from scratch to distinguish real and generated images using a separate discriminator loss</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=0.5(\mathrm{ReLU}(1-\mathrm{D}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))+\mathrm{ReLU}(1+\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t},\mathbf{x}_{t})=0.5(\mathrm{ReLU}(1-\mathrm{D}(\mathbf{x}_{t}))+\mathrm{ReLU}(1+\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t}))),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">encouraging <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\approx 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\mathbf{x}_{t})\approx 1</annotation></semantics></math> for real samples and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})\approx-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t})\approx-1</annotation></semantics></math> for fake (i.e., auto-encoded) samples, see Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ VQGAN ‣ 3.2 Open Source Models ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (selector positions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast\ast" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast\ast</annotation></semantics></math>).
We adapt the patch-based discriminator’s architecture from Esser et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, reducing the number of patches to 64, validated empirically (”our <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\;)</annotation></semantics></math>”).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/files/wm_training_architecture_wo7.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:336/445;" width="336" height="445" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning<span id="S3.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> of the </span>world model<span id="S3.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> for a single training sample consisting of a text index sequence <math id="S3.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and an image token index sequence <math id="S3.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cross-entropy loss <math id="S3.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{CE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>CE</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{CE}}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated between predicted probability distributions <math id="S3.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.128em" rspace="0.128em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> for image token indices and ground-truth indices <math id="S3.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning the World Model</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune the world model (WM) on a 4 fps <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">video</span> training dataset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>train</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), with each sample consisting of text token index sequence <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and an image patch token index sequence <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>, where each of the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> initial and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!14</annotation></semantics></math> future video frames is represented by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}\!=\!257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}\!=\!257</annotation></semantics></math> indices, see Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The input sequence <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{1}^{L}\!=\!(\mathbf{y}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{1}^{L}\!=\!(\mathbf{y}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math>, formed by embedding and concatenating <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>, is processed by <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{LWM}}\!=\!32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>LWM</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{LWM}}\!=\!32</annotation></semantics></math> decoder blocks under causal attention to predict the next image token index. Fine-tuning uses teacher forcing and cross-entropy loss over the predicted probability distributions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> for all target image patch tokens <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Due to the size of the WM, full fine-tuning is impractical, so we use the parameter-efficient LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> method, fine-tuning only a small set of additional adaptor weights for all linear and embedding layers, with full fine-tuning limited to normalization layer weights.
This results in 6.97B total model parameters, with only 2.39% of these being fine-tuneable.
To further reduce memory usage, frozen parameters are stored in the lower-precision format <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bfloat16</span>, while only fine-tunable parameters remain in <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FP32</span>. More details in Supplement B.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune our video decoder (VDEC) on the 4 fps <span id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">video</span> training dataset <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>train</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), using samples of 3 consecutive frames <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The frames are preprocessed and then tokenized separately using the fine-tuned and then frozen TOK, and jointly decoded to <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For optimization, we employ the image reconstruction loss <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and the generator component <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}</annotation></semantics></math> of a sequence-based GAN loss in analogy to (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), as video decoder loss</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}J^{\mathrm{VDEC}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1},\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1})\!=\!\sum_{\tau\!=\!t-1}^{t+1}J^{\mathrm{rec}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{\tau},\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\qquad\\
+\&gt;J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}).\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>VDEC</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mpadded style="width:0.327em;" width="0.327em"><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mpadded></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.716em;" width="0.716em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.158em" rspace="0.158em">=</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>rec</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em">+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}J^{\mathrm{VDEC}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1},\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1})\!=\!\sum_{\tau\!=\!t-1}^{t+1}J^{\mathrm{rec}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{\tau},\mathbf{x}_{\tau})\qquad\\
+\&gt;J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}).\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, we use the discriminator loss component <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{D-3D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1},\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{D-3D}}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1},\mathbf{x}_{t-1}^{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> after (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) to train a 3D patch-based discriminator <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}^{\mathrm{VDEC}}(\;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>VDEC</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}^{\mathrm{VDEC}}(\;)</annotation></semantics></math> from scratch, which jointly classifies sequences of three reconstructed frames <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{t-1}^{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We adopt our <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\;)</annotation></semantics></math> architecture from TOK+DEC training and inflate it to 3D, inspired by Yu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Amounts of videos and images<span id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> used in this work.
</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Notation</th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Data format</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Fps</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#train</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#val</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">#test</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">video</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">30</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">70k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">10k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">20k</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>train</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">video</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">70k</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">video</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">500</td>
<td id="S3.T1.6.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">-</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S3.T1.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">images</td>
