</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments and Discussion</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Berkeley Deep Drive Dataset (BDD100K) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> is the largest open source driving video dataset containing 100,000 videos, most of them with a duration of 40 seconds.
These have been recorded in various conditions, such as weather, lighting, and driving routes, captured at 30 fps with image size <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathring{H}\times\mathring{W}\!=\!720\times 1,280" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>̊</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>720</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1,280</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathring{H}\times\mathring{W}\!=\!720\times 1,280</annotation></semantics></math>.
As detailed in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the BDD100K video dataset <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises a training, validation, and test split, with 70k, 10k, and 20k videos, respectively.
For WM and VDEC fine-tuning and validation, we create a subset of 70k training videos <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>train</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and a subset of 500 validation videos, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{val-500}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{val-500}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}</annotation></semantics></math>, both subsampled to 4 fps.
BDD100K images <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a subset of
<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, contains one frame per video extracted just after 10 seconds.
For fine-tuning the image TOK (and image DEC), we created a larger custom image subset <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>BDDimg</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}</annotation></semantics></math> with about 538k training images, sampled at 0.2 fps from <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while we still validate on the official validation split <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{val}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi><mi>val</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{val}}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The BDD100K video training set <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}^{\mathrm{train}}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}</annotation></semantics></math> provides 778 hours of training data <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with approximately 84M unique images, which is only about 15% of the 5,100 hours of GAIA-1’s unpublished dataset with about 420M unique images</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess image reconstruction quality, we use PSNR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, SSIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, MS-SSIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>, and LPIPS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, comparing decoded images to ground-truth references.
For <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">generated</span> image/video frames, direct ground-truth comparison is not reasonable, accordingly, we report the Fréchet inception distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, the CLIP maximum mean discrepancy (CMMD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and for video data also the Fréchet video distance (FVD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.
The number of evaluated video frames is noted in the subscript (e.g., FVD<sub id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">14</sub>).
These perceptual metrics enable quality assessment of the WM and image/video decoder by evaluating predicted video frames while allowing them to diverge from corresponding ground-truth frames.
Thereby, we provide a <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">quantitative</span> analysis of image/video quality, while <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text">GAIA-1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> authors and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text">GAIA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> authors only report qualitative results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Details</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All our models are fine-tuned using the AdamW optimizer, distributing the batch across four <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Nvidia H100</span> 94GB GPUs.
Our TOK+DEC (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span>) is fine-tuned for 200k steps with a batch size of 80, comprising 2k steps of linear warm-up to an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and 150k steps of cosine decay to a final learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resulting in only 24 hours of fine-tuning</span>.
The discriminator loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and generator loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) are applied after 20k steps.
Our WM is fine-tuned for 28.3k steps with a batch size of 24, comprising 250 steps of linear warm-up to an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and 15k steps of cosine decay to a final learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resulting in 65 hours of fine-tuning</span>. Other configurations follow LLaMA-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>.
Our VDEC is fine-tuned for 100k steps with a batch size of 48, comprising 100 steps of linear warm-up to an initial learning rate of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> followed by a cosine decay schedule to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-7}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">resulting in 65 hours of fine-tuning.</span>
The discriminator loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{D-3D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{D-3D}</annotation></semantics></math> and generator loss <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G-3D}}</annotation></semantics></math> are applied after 2k steps.
All code is based on the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PyTorch<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">[</span><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">]</span></cite></span> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jax<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">[</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">]</span></cite></span> frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results and Discussion</h3>

<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Tokenizer<span id="S4.T2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>image decoder loss function<span id="S4.T2.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ablations: Quantitative results for <span id="S4.T2.9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">omitting</span> (-<math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math>) loss components during fine-tuning with the total loss <math id="S4.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), employing <math id="S4.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{D}</annotation></semantics></math>(<a href="#S3.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) for discriminator training, with <math id="S4.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and <math id="S4.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) using <span id="S4.T2.9.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINOv1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, evaluated on <math id="S4.T2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi><mi>val</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S4.T2.9.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> models (TOK + DEC) were fine-tuned on <math id="S4.T2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}^{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>BDDimg</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}^{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> for 200k iterations. Weights of omitted losses are set to zero. Visual examples for all experiments are shown in Supplement C.
Best results are in bold font, second best underlined. PSNR in (dB).
</span></span></figcaption>
