<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">b) Orig. RoI</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/vqgan_quali_files/quali_comp_zooms/v2/b1c9c847-3bda4659.jpg_rec_NOFT_crop_arrow.png" id="S4.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/685;" width="685" height="685" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">c) No fine-tuning</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_4">
<figure id="S4.F5.fig4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.15479v1/figures/vqgan_quali_files/quali_comp_zooms/v2/b1c9c847-3bda4659.jpg_rec_8x8_perc1_crop_oval.jpg" id="S4.F5.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/685;" width="685" height="685" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering">d) <span id="S4.F5.fig4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">proposed</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S4.F5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example</span> of a region of interest (RoI) for a transcoded (ENC+VQ+DEC = TOK+DEC) image from <math id="S4.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi><mi>val</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
(a) The RoI is marked in the original image <math id="S4.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (b) magnified for method comparison.
(c) Without domain-specific fine-tuning, the pre-trained <span id="S4.F5.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> produces poor reconstructions of automotive objects (here: parked cars).
(d) Proposed fine-tuning significantly improves image quality.
Best viewed on screen.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>,
we analyze and optimize the loss weights <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the important <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> contributions <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. We particularly show fine-tuning results using the discriminator from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, and in more detail <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">our</span> discriminator <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>) modified to classify fewer patches, each with an increased receptive field.
We finally propose and further use the TOK+DEC fine-tuning configuration of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), our <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}\!=\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}\!=\!1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}\!=\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}\!=\!1</annotation></semantics></math>, as it is overall strongest in the perceptual metrics and therefore shows highest potential for application in a generative time-predictive framework as ours.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows successful TOK+DEC fine-tuning, presenting an original image of an automotive scene (Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>a), a zoomed-in region of interest highlighting parked cars (Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>b), and corresponding transcoded model outputs (<a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>c,d). The non-fine-tuned <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> (TOK+DEC) exhibits poor reconstructions of car parts (Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>c).
Fine-tuning with our proposed configuration from Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> restores structure and details, thereby improving perceptual quality.
More ablation results and visual examples are in Supplement C.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">World Model</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ World Model ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the overall system performance with WM, but still using the image decoder DEC. Different teacher models are used for self-supervised learning loss <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#S3.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) during TOK + DEC fine-tuning. Evaluation is performed on video data <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!14</annotation></semantics></math> predicted frames based on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> conditioning frames.
While GAIA-1 uses a probably larger <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINO</span> (Version 1) for <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math>, our best FVD score is achieved with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>SSL</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>DINOv2</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This demonstrates the effectiveness of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINOv2</span>’s representations over <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINO</span> or no <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> guidance, motivating its use as the teacher for our finally proposed TOK+DEC fine-tuning, therefore used in all consecutive experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Distillation teacher model<span id="S4.T4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ablations: Quantitative results for different teacher models used with <math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> during fine-tuning of the tokenizer (TOK) and image decoder (DEC). Results were calculated for <math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.128em">=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!14</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}</annotation></semantics></math> subset.
Best results are in bold font.
Top-<math id="S4.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> value provided.
</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">TOK/DEC</th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Validation: TOK/WM/DEC</th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r">fine-tuned with</th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r"><math id="S4.T4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">FID<sub id="S4.T4.6.2.3.1" class="ltx_sub">14</sub> <math id="S4.T4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">CMMD<sub id="S4.T4.6.2.4.1" class="ltx_sub">14</sub> <math id="S4.T4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column">FVD<sub id="S4.T4.6.2.5.1" class="ltx_sub">14</sub> <math id="S4.T4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">No <math id="S4.T4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">50</th>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">14.65</td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.105</td>
<td id="S4.T4.6.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">188.59</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
