<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ World Model ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we investigate the world model’s performance depending on the top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> hyperparameter, which controls the breadth of token selection during inference.
We show TOK+DEC and TOK+VDEC as reference, which perform no prediction (i.e., no WM), instead, here, we only transcode the video frames.
VDEC is based on DEC trained with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>SSL</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>DINOv2</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and our <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\;)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We observe that temporal prediction by the WM of course harms the perceptual metrics, but values in the range <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=10...1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=10...1000</annotation></semantics></math> improve the metrics. For our finally proposed OpenViGA system, we recommend <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1000</annotation></semantics></math> as this choice provides the best perceptive video quality (FVD<sub id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub">14</sub>).
Visual examples of generated videos are given in the Supplement D.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Image/Video Decoder</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5 Limitations ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents our final quantitative comparison of the image decoder (DEC) and the video decoder (VDEC) for temporal predictive video generation using the WM.
We compare results of both systems using their strongest top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> results in terms of FVD<sub id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sub">14</sub>.
We observe that using our VDEC yields superior FVD<sub id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_sub">14</sub> performance compared to our DEC (132.16 vs. 160.48), justifying its adoption in our finally proposed OpenViGA system.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our 3D CNN video decoder, trained with GAN and reconstruction losses, likely produces lower-quality frames than modern diffusion-based methods. However, it offers a simple-to-train solution that effectively handles quantized latent image patch tokens and seamlessly integrates into our system. While also being effective in its frame-by-frame processing, extending its temporal context might allow to enhance visual coherence beyond our current achievements.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T6.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="S5.T6.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Image/video decoder<span id="S5.T6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>final results with strongest top-<math id="S5.T6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.T6.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Quantitative results for deploying the <span id="S5.T6.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image</span> decoder (DEC) or <span id="S5.T6.8.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">video</span> decoder (VDEC) for generating video frames from WM output. Results were calculated for <math id="S5.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.128em">=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!14</annotation></semantics></math> on the <math id="S5.T6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDvid</mi><mrow><mi>val</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid}}^{\mathrm{val-500}}</annotation></semantics></math> subset. VDEC is based on DEC trained with <math id="S5.T6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>SSL</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>DINOv2</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL-DINOv2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. OpenViGA employs TOK/WM/VDEC. Top-<math id="S5.T6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> value provided.
Best results are in bold font.
</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S5.T6.9" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T6.9.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.9.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">System</th>
