<section id="S1a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">A </span>Details on used Open Source Models</h2>

<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">VQGAN</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The vector-quantized generative adversarial network (<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span>) combines the strengths of vector quantization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and GANs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> to generate high-quality images with compact latent representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
The architecture consists of an encoder, a vector quantization module, and decoder, utilizing convolutional layers for image compression and reconstruction.
Without skip connections between encoder and decoder, both can be applied independently.
The open-source <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> model, which we leverage to initialize our fine-tuning process, has a spatial compression factor of 16, encoding to a latent dimension of <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\!=\!64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\!=\!64</annotation></semantics></math>.
The vector quantizer (VQ) relies on nearest-neighbor lookup, using a vocabulary size <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\!=\!8192\!=\!2^{13}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>13</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\!=\!8192\!=\!2^{13}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The blocks ENC, VQ (jointly being TOK), and DEC utilize 58.7M, 532.4k, and 87M model parameters, respectively.
Consequently, our TOK comprises 59.2M parameters, only about 20% of the 300M parameters in GAIA-1’s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> tokenizer.
The weights were originally optimized for 2.5M steps on real-world images of 256x256 resolution from various domains, yielding a general-purpose <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">However, deploying it for front-facing camera images of automotive driving scenes, the model exhibits limited reconstruction quality.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LWM</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The large world model (<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span>) is a multimodal autoregressive decoder-only transformer capable of processing sequences as long as 1M tokens of both video and text modalities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span> is based on <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> in its 7B parameter variant.
Accordingly, it employs <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{LWM}}\!=\!32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>LWM</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{LWM}}\!=\!32</annotation></semantics></math> decoder blocks and 32 heads in the multi-head attention. The internal feature dimension <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is 4,096.
The open-source <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM-Chat-1M</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> model variant is used to initialize our WM.
The authors initialized from <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LLaMA-2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> and trained on language tasks with text sequences of progressively increasing lengths up to 1M tokens.
In a second stage, they trained the <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span> on four equally weighted vision-language tasks: image understanding, video understanding, text-image generation, and text-video generation.
The authors tokenized images and videos using the general-purpose <span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> ENC+VQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, also discussed above.
Approximately 495B language-vision tokens were utilized, aggregated from datasets such as LAION-2B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> and WebVid-10M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, primarily consisting of captioned images and videos from diverse domains obtained via web scraping.
<span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">However, no dedicated driving video dataset containing recordings from forward-facing cameras is utilized.
Also, the task of predicting future latent frames based on initial latent frames was not addressed in </span><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> training.
Moreover, </span><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LWM</span><span id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> was trained with 256 tokens per image defining the image tokenizer output size and thus limits the video frame resolution, which we streamline to fit to the BDD100K automotive dataset.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">B </span>Fine-Tuning the World Model</h2>

<figure id="S2.T7" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S2.T7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 7</span>: </span><span id="S2.T7.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Tokenizer<span id="S2.T7.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> and </span>image decoder discriminator and loss weight<span id="S2.T7.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> ablations: Quantitative results for using our <math id="S2.T7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.220em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D}(\&gt;)</annotation></semantics></math> with varying weights <math id="S2.T7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.T7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> for perceptual loss and generator loss during fine-tuning with the total loss <math id="S2.T7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math>, employing <math id="S2.T7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>D</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> for discriminator training, with <math id="S2.T7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> using <span id="S2.T7.8.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">DINOv1</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, evaluated on <math id="S2.T7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>BDDimg</mi><mi>val</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg}}^{\mathrm{val}}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.T7.8.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> models (TOK + DEC) were fine-tuned on <math id="S2.T7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}^{\mathrm{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>BDDimg</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">s</mi></mrow><mi>train</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDimg/5s}}^{\mathrm{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> for 200k iterations. Best results are in bold font, second best underlined. PSNR in (dB).
</span></span></figcaption>
<table id="S2.T7.9" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
