<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fine-Tuning the World Model</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fine-tune the world model (WM) on a 4 fps <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">video</span> training dataset <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>BDDvid</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>train</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{BDDvid-4fps}}^{\mathrm{train-70k}}</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Fine-Tuning the Video Decoder ‣ 3.3 Our Streamlining and Fine-Tuning ‣ 3 OpenViGA ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> in the main paper), as shown in Fig. 4 in the main paper for a single training sample.
The sample consists of a text token index sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}\!=\!(c_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and an image patch token index sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math>, which corresponds to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!=\!2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!=\!2</annotation></semantics></math> initial video frames and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\!=\!16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\!=\!16</annotation></semantics></math> future video frames, each represented by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}\!=\!257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}\!=\!257</annotation></semantics></math> indices.
Each index <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\nu}\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\nu}\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the set of indices <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}\!=\!\{0,1,...,K\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\!=\!\{0,1,...,K\}</annotation></semantics></math>, represents either an entry from the image tokenizer’s codebook <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CB}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>CB</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CB}</annotation></semantics></math>, or an end-of-frame token (<math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\nu}\!=\!0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\nu}\!=\!0</annotation></semantics></math>).
As model input, the text index sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and the image token index sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1}\!=\!(k_{\nu})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1}\!=\!(k_{\nu})</annotation></semantics></math> that omits the last ground-truth index are both embedded with dedicated layers into a <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\!=\!4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\!=\!4096</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional space before being concatenated.
The resulting input sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{y}_{1}^{L}\!=\!(\mathbf{y}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>L</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{y}_{1}^{L}\!=\!(\mathbf{y}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> of length <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\!=\!M+(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\!=\!M+(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}-1</annotation></semantics></math> is then processed by the WM’s <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N^{\mathrm{LWM}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>N</mi><mi>LWM</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N^{\mathrm{LWM}}</annotation></semantics></math> decoder blocks.
To fine-tune for next-token prediction, decoder blocks apply causal attention to mask future tokens, forcing the WM to rely solely on preceding and current elements of the (ground-truth) input sequence.
This teacher-forcing mechanism differs from autoregressive inference, which relies on prior index predictions.
Next, a root mean square layer normalization (RMS-Norm) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> and an <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{FC(}K+1\mathrm{)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>FC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{FC(}K+1\mathrm{)}</annotation></semantics></math> layer with softmax activation are applied to each position in the sequence.
The output sequence <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{2}^{L+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{2}^{L+1}</annotation></semantics></math> comprises <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> discrete probability distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\ell}\in[0,1]^{K+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\ell}\in[0,1]^{K+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, each estimating the token probabilities at ground-truth sequence position <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell\in\mathcal{L}\!=\!\{2,...,L\!+\!1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell\in\mathcal{L}\!=\!\{2,...,L\!+\!1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For loss calculation, we cut this sequence to contain only the predicted probability distributions <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐏</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{M+1}^{L+1}\!=\!(\mathbf{P}_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> for <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">image patch</span> token indices corresponding to the ground-truth image patch token indices <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!k_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{1}^{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\!=\!k_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>, and optimize using their respective cross-entropy loss</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J^{\mathrm{CE}}=-\frac{1}{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{I}}\mathrm{log}P(k_{\nu}|c_{1}^{M},k_{1}^{\nu-1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>CE</mi></msup><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mfrac><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>log</mi><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{CE}}=-\frac{1}{(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}}\sum_{\nu\in\mathcal{I}}\mathrm{log}P(k_{\nu}|c_{1}^{M},k_{1}^{\nu-1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}\!=\!\{1,2,...,(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}\!=\!\{1,2,...,(T+N)\cdot n^{\prime}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to iterate over all image token indices in the target sequence and the estimated probability for the ground-truth token index <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(k_{\nu}|c_{1}^{M},k_{1}^{\nu-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(k_{\nu}|c_{1}^{M},k_{1}^{\nu-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the size of the WM, full fine-tuning is impractical, so we use the parameter-efficient LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> method, fine-tuning only a small set of additional adaptor weights for all linear and embedding layers, with full fine-tuning limited to normalization layer weights.
This results in 6.97B total model parameters, with only 2.39% of these being fine-tuneable.
To further reduce memory usage, frozen parameters are stored in the lower-precision format <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bfloat16</span>, while only fine-tunable parameters remain in <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1a.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FP32</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3a" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">C </span>Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder</h2>

<div id="S3a.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3a.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> shows visual examples of an original validation image (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>a), the transcoded (TOK+DEC) output using the pre-trained, not fine-tuned <span id="S3a.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VQGAN</span> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>b) and various reconstructions for fine-tuned models corresponding to Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> from the main paper.
We find the results of the fine-tuning with <math id="S3a.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> most visually convincing in terms of perceptual image quality. which is in line with the quantitative evaluations.
Omitting the self-supervised learning component <math id="S3a.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{SSL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>SSL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{SSL}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>d) appears to also produce good results, although the image quality is slightly less refined.
Excluding the perceptual loss term <math id="S3a.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>e) results in a noticeable degradation in perceptual quality, with visible reconstruction artifacts.
Removing the <math id="S3a.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{L2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>L2</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{L2}}</annotation></semantics></math> loss term (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>f) leads to sharp but excessively detailed image regions compared to <math id="S3a.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, omitting the generator component of the GAN loss <math id="S3a.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ C Fine-Tuning the Tokenizer and Image Decoder ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>g)clearly causes blurred reconstructions that lack fine details.
Table <a href="#S2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B Fine-Tuning the World Model ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> extends Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Image Tokenizer ‣ 4.2 Results and Discussion ‣ 4 Experiments and Discussion ‣ OpenViGA: Video Generation for Automotive Driving Scenesby Streamlining and Fine-Tuning Open Source Models with Public Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> from the main paper. We show more experiments on loss weights <math id="S3a.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3a.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>λ</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the important <math id="S3a.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>total</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> contributions <math id="S3a.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3a.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\mathrm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\mathrm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S3.F6.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
